   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DATA SUPPORT MENA 2021</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MENA region</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NLRC, through its 510 initiative provides remote technical assistance to RCRC national societies. IFRC will be involved to coordinate the support provided to countries in the MENA region and will itself also receive data &amp; digital support from NLRC/510.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NLRC/510
IFRC MENA region
RCRC NS in the MENA region</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The support provided is flexible and demand driven. It also follows a human centered process, ensuring that client needs and the local context drive the definition of NLRC&apos;s support. The identified needs must be realistic in scope, must be within the ability of the NLRC to answer and finally, there must be a clear commitment from the recipient NS to engage proactively with NLRC throughout the project. The countries that will ultimately receive support through this project have not yet been determined but are expected to primarily be in the MENA (Middle East and Northern Africa) regions. Based on the knowledge collected and the changing dynamic of the pandemic, we anticipate that recipient national societies will request data and digital support in the following (generic) areas:
1.	Continuation of crucial data &amp; digital support for NS for emergency support products
2.	Support NS data and digital support for Covid vaccination campaign. 
3.	Compounding disasters:  disaster response complicated by to the Covid pandemic
4.	Cash IM support</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the 510 Data &amp; Digital Team NLRC assists 19 RCRC National Societies in their short-term preparedness and response to COVID-19 and supported their operational continuity, through data and digital. This support was made possible through a 12-month block funded project that started implementation on 1 April 2020. At the time of writing (2 months before the end of the project) several countries are encountering a renewed increase of infections. New uncertainties are introduced by the virus mutations, underlining that the Covid response phase is far from over. With the approval of several vaccines and the start of vaccination campaigns in countries around the world, the focus is beginning to shift from response to recovery. The road however remains long and based on the achievements of the 2020 global COVID program and the feedback received the added value of continued data and digital support beyond the current project&apos;s end date, 31 March 2021, is clear.</narrative>
  </description>
